        {
          "id": "A",
          "heading": "What it looks like",
          "body": "Delirium comes on over hours or days, and it fluctuates. A patient who was oriented at ten in the morning and confused at four in the afternoon has delirium until something else is proved.\n\nAttention is the part that is always affected. Ask the patient to name the months of the year backwards from December; somebody with delirium loses the thread partway and cannot pick it up again.\n\nTwo forms exist. The overactive form is restless, agitated and hard to miss. The quiet form is withdrawn, drowsy and answers in single words, and it is the one that goes unrecognised for days.\n\nThe quiet form carries the worse outcome of the two. It is missed because a patient who lies still and asks for nothing is read as comfortable, and because nobody is called to a bedside that is silent.\n\nDelirium is not dementia. Dementia arrives over months and does not fluctuate through a single day, and a patient may of course have both. Where the history is unclear, treat it as delirium and look for a cause.\n\nAsk the family what this person was like a week ago. They will answer a question the notes cannot."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "heading": "Looking for the cause",
          "body": "There is almost never one cause. Four or five small things together are the usual picture, and removing any one of them may be enough to turn the patient round.\n\nStart with pain, constipation and a full bladder. All three are common, all three are easily missed in somebody who cannot explain themselves, and all three are treatable within the hour.\n\nInfection, dehydration and a low sodium are the next places to look. Send urine only where there are symptoms; treating a positive dipstick in an older patient with no symptoms causes more harm than it prevents.\n\nReview every medicine on the chart, and pay attention to what has been started in the last week. Sedatives, strong painkillers, drugs for bladder symptoms and drugs for nausea all appear repeatedly.\n\nAsk about alcohol, and ask about the medicines the patient takes at home that nobody has prescribed here.\nWithdrawal begins on the second or third day of an admission, which is exactly when a new confusion is blamed on the ward instead."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "heading": "Caring for the patient",
          "body": "Almost all of the treatment is not a drug. Light, quiet, a clock the patient can see, and the same faces where the roster allows are worth more than anything on the chart.\n\nBring in the glasses and the hearing aid. A patient who cannot see or hear the ward is being asked to make sense of it with the evidence removed.\n\nMove the patient as little as the ward allows. Every change of bay resets whatever orientation had been built up, and a night move is worse than a daytime one.\n\nDo not correct a frightened patient repeatedly. Acknowledge the feeling, say who you are and where they are, and say it the same way each time.\n\nMedication is a last resort, for a patient at real risk of harming themselves or somebody else, and only after everything above has been tried. The dose is the smallest available, reviewed daily, and stopped rather than continued out of the ward.\n\nInvolve the family in the care where they want to be involved. A familiar voice at the bedside settles more patients than any prescription written on this ward.\n\nProtect the night. Observations that can wait until morning are moved, lights are lowered on the whole bay rather than at one bed, and a patient who is awake is offered a drink and a chair by the window instead of being put back to bed in the dark.\n\nWrite down what worked. The approach that calmed this patient at three in the morning is information the next shift cannot guess, and it is lost unless somebody records it before going home."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "heading": "After the episode",
          "body": "Delirium is not a harmless episode that passes. It predicts a longer stay, a greater chance of a move into residential care, and a higher mortality in the year that follows.\n\nRecovery is slow. Some patients are back to themselves in days; others are still not right at six weeks, and a proportion never return fully to the level they were at before.\n\nWrite the episode in the discharge summary with what was found and what was stopped. A medicine stopped here and restarted at the surgery next month has taught nobody anything.\n\nTalk to the patient about it afterwards, where they are able. Many remember fragments and find them frightening, and being told that what they remember was an illness helps more than being told to forget it.\n\nScreen for delirium on admission for anybody over sixty-five, and again whenever the nursing staff report that a patient seems different. That report is the most reliable single test on the ward."
        }
