        {
          "id": "A",
          "heading": "Deciding to catheterise",
          "body": "The safest catheter is the one that is never inserted. Every day a catheter stays in adds to the chance of infection, and that chance rises steeply after the first week.\n\nRetention, accurate measurement in a critically ill patient, and certain operations are reasons. Incontinence on its own is not, and the convenience of the staff is never one.\n\nWrite the reason for insertion in the notes on the day it goes in, together with the planned removal date.\nA catheter with no recorded reason is a catheter nobody will feel able to take out.\n\nConsider the alternatives first, and record which were considered. A sheath, a bottle within reach, timed assistance to the toilet, or a bedside commode will meet the need for a great many patients.\n\nReview the reason every single day on the ward round, and say the answer out loud. A silent review is indistinguishable from no review at all, and most catheters that stay too long stay because the question stopped being asked rather than because anybody decided to keep them."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "heading": "Putting it in and looking after it",
          "body": "Insertion is a sterile procedure carried out by somebody trained to do it, with the hands cleaned immediately beforehand and gloves changed between cleaning the patient and handling the catheter.\n\nChoose the smallest size that will drain, and inflate the balloon only after urine is seen. A balloon inflated in the urethra causes an injury that takes months to settle.\n\nThe drainage bag stays below the level of the bladder at all times, and off the floor. A bag lifted above the bladder to carry a patient across a corridor runs its contents back the way they came.\n\nThe system is closed and is not disconnected to take samples, to empty it or to move the patient. Each disconnection is an opening, and the tubing does not seal itself again afterwards.\n\nEmpty the bag before it is full, using a clean container for that patient alone, and clean the outlet afterwards. A container carried between two beds carries more than urine.\n\nWash the area around the catheter daily with soap and water. Antiseptics, creams and powders have not been shown to help and several of them irritate the skin."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "heading": "Recognising an infection",
          "body": "Cloudy urine and a strong smell are not an infection. They are usually concentration, and treating them has filled wards with resistant organisms.\n\nLook instead for fever, new pain over the bladder or the loin, rigors, or a sudden confusion in an older patient with nothing else to explain it.\n\nDo not send urine from a patient with no symptoms, and do not treat a positive result in one. Every long-term catheter grows organisms; that is colonisation, and it is not a disease.\n\nWhere a sample is genuinely needed, take it from the sampling port after cleaning it, and never from the drainage bag. Urine that has been standing in a bag tells you about the bag.\n\nSend the sample before the first dose of an antibiotic, not after it. An hour of waiting for the porter costs less than a culture that grows nothing and leaves the treatment unguided for a week.\n\nReplace the catheter when an infection is being treated, and take the sample from the new one. The old catheter carries a layer that antibiotics do not reach."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "heading": "Taking it out",
          "body": "Removal is a nursing decision on most wards, made against an agreed protocol, and it does not have to wait for a ward round.\n\nTake it out early in the day rather than in the evening. A patient who cannot pass urine afterwards is then found by a full team rather than by a single night nurse.\n\nDeflate the balloon passively and let it empty on its own. Drawing the water out actively leaves a ridge that hurts on the way past.\n\nRecord the time of removal, and watch for the first passing of urine. Six hours without any is the point at which somebody is told, and a bladder scan is more use at that moment than another hour of waiting.\n\nWarn the patient that some stinging and some urgency are normal for a day or two. A patient who has not been warned will assume an infection and will ask for antibiotics nobody needs to prescribe.\n\nOffer the toilet at intervals for the rest of that day rather than waiting to be asked. Somebody who has had a catheter for a week has lost the habit of noticing a full bladder, and the first hours after removal are when a preventable retention happens.\n\nWhere a catheter has to go back in, write down why the first attempt at removal failed. Two ward rounds later that sentence is the difference between trying again and assuming it cannot be done."
        }
