        {
          "id": "A",
          "heading": "Knowing the person",
          "body": "Ask the family to fill in the document the ward keeps for this purpose, and read it before the first conversation rather than after the first difficulty.\n\nWhat the person prefers to be called, what they did for a living, what frightens them, how they take their tea and what settles them at night are the details that make the difference.\n\nAsk what this person was like a fortnight ago. A hospital sees somebody at their worst and assumes it is how they always are.\n\nPut the essential facts where the whole team will see them, and update them when something is learned. A document filed in a folder helps nobody at two in the morning.\n\nSpeak to the patient rather than about them. A relative in the room is not a reason to stop addressing the person whose admission this is.\n\nRemember that dementia is not one condition and does not run one course. Two patients with the same diagnosis may need entirely different approaches on the same bay."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "heading": "Talking and being understood",
          "body": "Come into view before you speak, say who you are and why you are there, and say it the same way each time.\nApproaching from behind startles people and the conversation begins badly.\n\nUse short sentences and one idea at a time. Two instructions joined by the word and will usually lose the second one.\n\nAsk questions that can be answered with a yes or a no where the answer matters, and leave a longer silence than feels comfortable before filling it in yourself.\n\nDo not argue with what the person believes. Where somebody is waiting for a mother who died forty years ago, ask about her rather than correcting the fact.\n\nWatch what your own face and hands are doing. Tone and expression are understood long after the words stop being understood, and a hurried manner is read accurately every time.\n\nWhere speech has largely gone, look for meaning in behaviour. Pulling at clothing, calling out and pacing are communication and usually mean pain, needing the toilet, thirst or fear."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "heading": "The ward around them",
          "body": "Noise, glare, echo and constant movement all make things worse. A bay by the door with a trolley passing every few minutes is the worst bed on the ward for this patient.\n\nAvoid moving the patient between wards, and avoid moving them at night above all. Each move undoes everything that has been learned about where they are.\n\nMake the toilet easy to find and easy to recognise, and take the person regularly rather than waiting to be asked. A great deal of what is written down as agitation is a full bladder.\n\nKeep the glasses, the hearing aid and the dentures with the patient and in use, and check the batteries.\nThese are the three items most often lost during an admission.\n\nProtect sleep and protect the routine. Observations at three in the morning that could be done at six are not free of cost.\n\nLet people stay outside visiting hours where they want to, and let them help with meals and with washing if that is what they and the patient prefer."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "heading": "Safety and decisions",
          "body": "Assess capacity for the particular decision in front of you and at that moment. A patient may be unable to decide about an operation and perfectly able to decide what to wear.\n\nWhere a person lacks capacity, the decision is made in their best interests, and that is not the same as the safest option or the one the family prefers.\n\nFind out whether anybody holds a formal legal authority to decide, and ask to see it rather than accepting that it exists.\n\nFalls, poor eating and delirium are the three things most likely to harm this patient during the admission, and delirium on top of dementia is common and often missed.\n\nDo not restrain and do not sedate for the convenience of the ward. Look for what is causing the behaviour first, because there almost always is one.\n\nPlan the discharge from the first day and involve the family in it. A patient with dementia who stays longer than they need to will leave less able than they arrived.\n\nWrite the discharge letter so that whoever reads it next knows the person and not only the illness. What they can do for themselves, what help they need and what settles them belong in it alongside the tablets.\n\nAsk the carer directly how they are managing, and ask when the patient is not in the room. Many will say everything is fine in front of a relative they are exhausted by and would never criticise.\n\nCheck that the support at home is actually in place on the day, not promised for the following week. A discharge arranged around a service that begins on Monday leaves a weekend that nobody has planned for."
        }
